 "fieldNotes": "The track was trimmed because there was no trailhead near the start or end. Where did the ride start and finish, and is either end near home? If so, this needs the from-home treatment (no map, route or distances).\nSkunk Beach or Cove on Lake Tahoe\n\nWhere was this? Which stretch of shore and trailhead or public-land name can be used?\nSkunk Harbor\n\nWere the aerial photos taken with a drone? If so, OK to say so, and was it flown where drones are allowed?\nYes and yes.\n\nWas anyone else along (Marley, your wife, others)? Nothing in the notes says.\nYes. Marley.\n\nPhotos 01-03 and 06/08 look like the same shots with different edits. Keep all, or one of each?\nThose are raw HDR images that are to be merged via or new protocol.\n\nDo you know what the line of posts in the water (07) and the buildings in the cove are? Worth a line if so.\nAn old dock from long ago.\n\nWas the 22 minutes moving out of two hours mostly the photo stop, or something else (tracker left running, a walk, a swim)?\nThe trip up and down on my bike.\n\nAny title, notes or a moment from the day you want in the story? The draft is built only from the figures and photos.\nMornings with Marley.",
 "post": {
  "title": "Clear water in March",
  "body": "I started rolling just before nine on March 14. It was a short ride, about three miles with a little under six hundred feet of climbing, and only twenty-two minutes of it was actually moving. The whole outing took a bit over two hours.\n\nThe pictures explain where the rest of the time went. They're all taken from above the shoreline, between about half past nine and a quarter past ten. There was still snow in patches under the pines and between the granite, but the light was bright and the shadows of the trees reached a long way out over the water.\n\nThe water is what I keep looking at. Straight down, the boulders under the surface show every edge, brown and green against that turquoise, and the shallows fade into deep blue farther out. Looking along the shore, the forest runs right down to the rocks, and snowy mountains sit across the lake in the distance.\n\nThe last shot, a little after ten, shows a small cove with a few old buildings back in the trees and a line of old posts standing in the water. I don't know their story yet.\n\nThe high point of the ride was about sixty-eight hundred feet, and the top speed was around twenty-six miles an hour, which on a three-mile ride says something about how the downhill went."
 },
 "episode": {
  "title": "Three miles and a long look down",
  "script": "Just before nine on a March morning, Christian set off on a ride by Lake Tahoe.\n\nNot a long one, now. About three miles. A little under six hundred feet of climbing, and the same back down. The moving part took twenty-two minutes.\n\nThe whole morning took a little over two hours. And sure, you'll see why.\n\n***\n\nThe photos tell you exactly where the time went. They start at half past nine, and every last one of them is taken from above.\n\nLook straight down, and the lake is so clear the boulders under the surface seem to float in the air. Brown and green stones, one beside the next, and the water around them going from turquoise to a deep, deep blue. Go hálainn. Beautiful.\n\nThere was still snow that morning. Patches of it under the pines, and white pockets tucked in between the granite on the shore. The trees threw long shadows right out across the water.\n\nAlong the shore, the forest runs all the way down to the rocks. And far across the lake, the mountains were still white.\n\n***\n\nThe last picture comes a little after ten. A small cove. A couple of old buildings, back in the trees. And standing in the shallows, a line of old posts, leading out into the water.\n\nWhat they once held up, the photo doesn't say. Which would make you wonder, wouldn't it?\n\n***\n\nThen it was back on the bike. Top speed that day, about twenty-six miles an hour. On a three-mile ride, that's most of the story of the way down. Whee.\n\nThree miles of riding. Nearly two hours of looking. Some mornings, the ratio is exactly right.",
